# Milestone: M3
# Covers: tag.stop() stops a sleeping strand tagged with that tag;
#         concurrent variants: stop before vs after strand starts sleeping.
# blocked: `a & b` runs `b` only after `a` has finished, so in `{sleep(100ms); echo("No")} & t.stop()` the echo happens before the stop.
# matrix-row: tag-stop
#
# Rewritten from legacy tests/2.x/stop.chk for the host driver, with one
# tag per case so neither case can be stopped by the other's stop.  On
# the line-at-a-time path a strand parked on sleep(100ms) is still parked
# when the line returns, so `echo("No")` would be absent whether or not
# the stop worked.  Here the virtual clock is moved past the sleep and the
# scheduler stepped: the control scope, which nobody stops, DOES echo,
# and the two stopped ones must not.  The expected block below is the
# legacy behaviour; on this tree the first stopped case prints `*** No a`
# after the clock moves, because its stop arm only starts once the
# sleeping arm is done.
#
# A chunk hands back once nothing is READY, so a chunk still parked in
# its tag scope returns nil rather than its trailing value.
## timeout: 30
## host: run var a = Tag.new()
## host: run var b = Tag.new()
## host: run var ctl = Tag.new()
## host: run ctl: { {sleep(100ms); echo("Yes")} & 1 }; 0
## host: run a: { {sleep(100ms); echo("No a")} & a.stop() }; 0
## host: run b: { b.stop() & {sleep(100ms); echo("No b")} }; 0
## host: step 1000
## host: advance-clock 200
## host: step 1000
## host: live-work
[00000000] <Tag>
[00000000] <Tag>
[00000000] <Tag>
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] step: WAKE_AT
[00000000] *** Yes
[00000000] step: QUIESCENT
[00000000] live-work: false
